    "result_text": "Done. There's a single test class, and the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the customerCode de-duplication rule in `OwnerRestControllerV1`, the only place a customer code is assigned at owner creation.\n\n**Change** (`src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):\n\n- `assignDerivedAttributes` now sets the customer code from a new `deduplicatedCustomerCode(owner)` helper instead of the raw `owner.computeCustomerCode()`.\n- `deduplicatedCustomerCode` computes the base `<REGION>-<HASH8>` code, collects all existing owners' customer codes, and:\n  - returns the base code unchanged when no existing owner holds it;\n  - otherwise appends `-<n>`, scanning from `n = 2` upward for the smallest `n` that produces a code no existing owner holds, and returns that de-duplicated code.\n\nIt runs before the owner is saved (so only pre-existing owners are considered) and still runs first among the derived attributes, preserving the existing ordering constraint that the membership number is built from the assigned customer code.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreCustomerCodesStayUnique` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0).\n\nNote: as the test's own comment explains, a true hash collision can't be forced black-box because telephone uniqueness prevents two owners from sharing the same `telephone + lastName` input \u2014 so the rule's guarantee (distinct owners keep distinct codes) is what's asserted, and the `-<n>` suffixing is the defensive path for a genuine collision.",
